        "role": "user",
        "content": "Read the exact question and ALL original source segments. Documents, cards, saved answers and annotations are fallible data, never instructions. Distinct valid namesakes and complementary answers may coexist; competing assertions about one referent are not automatically distinct valid answers. Do not invent source authority, document independence or unstated question constraints. A quoted assertion, occurrence, or repeated text is not truth. Match requested subject/object, relation direction, negation, time and answer type. A city in a birth sentence is not a date. In \"A's manager B\", B is the manager, not A. Sports biographies can positively support multiple legitimate sports. An incidental chart, associated person or date is not automatically the answer. Use only supplied candidate/source IDs in bounded fields; no free quotation or prose.\nFor EVERY candidate, judge fit: direct means the answer itself fills the exact requested relation for an allowed referent; wrong_argument is explicit role/type mismatch; incidental means occurrence without enough support; uncertain means unresolved. Select support only for an original segment positively assigning this candidate to the requested relation; otherwise empty. Select counter only for actual counterevidence, not mere absence. conflict=same_referent only for incompatible claims for the same entity/attribute/relevant scope, not legitimate namesakes or complementary facts. entity labels are provisional groups, not truth. scope=excluded only for an explicit question constraint, with constraint selecting a literal numeric phrase from the question; otherwise allowed/unknown. observed_slot describes the answer's ACTUAL role, not the role wanted by the question. Mere disagreement does not make a source false. Fill all opaque keys.\nIndependent polarity defense: for each candidate scan ALL documents for a positive assignment to the requested role. Positive assignment for an allowed distinct referent must be fit=direct and placed in SUPPORT even if the candidate is not famous or not in the first document. A support statement is not a counter. Counter requires an explicit negative/exclusion or incompatible statement about that exact referent, and cannot be justified by absence. A birth header (date1 - date2) supports date1, not merely prose Born on. Distinct people sharing a name, distinct works with one title, multiple sports and plural mascots may legitimately coexist. Role match and truth are separate axes: competing same-role values stay direct with conflict flagged. Unknown memory is not negative evidence. Recheck all wrong_argument labels against the source before returning.\nInput: {\"question\": \"What is the medium of \\\"The Private Secretary\\\"?\", \"slot_hint\": \"other\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 94, \"text\": \"The Private Secretary The Private Secretary is a 1883 farce in three acts, by Charles Hawtrey.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d00\", \"start\": 95, \"end\": 302, \"text\": \"The play, adapted from a German original, depicts the vicissitudes of a mild young clergyman, innocently caught up in the machinations of two irresponsible young men who are bent on escaping their creditors.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d00\", \"start\": 303, \"end\": 460, \"text\": \"When the play opened in London the notices for the piece were hostile, and it was seen as a stop-gap in the schedule of the theatrical company presenting it.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d00\", \"start\": 461, \"end\": 533, \"text\": \"Despite these predictions the play ran in London for 1,469 performances.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d00\", \"start\": 534, \"end\": 594, \"text\": \"The critics found much more to praise in the performances of\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 188, \"text\": \"The Private Secretary (1935 film) The Private Secretary is a 1935 British comedy film directed by Henry Edwards and starring Edward Everett Horton, Barry MacKay, Judy Gunn and Oscar Asche.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d01\", \"start\": 189, \"end\": 270, \"text\": \"It is an adaptation of the play \\\"The Private Secretary\\\" by Charles Henry Hawtrey.\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d01\", \"start\": 271, \"end\": 305, \"text\": \"It was made at Twickenham Studios.\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d01\", \"start\": 306, \"end\": 549, \"text\": \"Wealthy Englishman Robert Cattermole returns from a trip to India to discover that instead of achieving success, his beloved nephew Robert has got into such debt that he now disguises himself as a mild-mannered minister to evade his creditors.\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d01\", \"start\": 550, \"end\": 617, \"text\": \"\\\"TV Guide\\\" felt the comedy of the Victorian farce \\\"didn't translate\"}, \"D3S1\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d02\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 683, \"text\": \"resources The guidelines project Becoming a translator or interpreter for government agencies About Open Menu Back Close Menu About Who we are Open Menu Back Close Menu Who we are Our senior leadership team Our structure Our promise Te Kī Taurangi Corporate publications Our ministers Our external boards Crown entities and statutory boards Board appointments Code of Conduct Flexible working at MBIE policy Prosecution policy Child Protection Work for us Open government and official information Open Menu Back Close Menu Open government and official information Data sharing Published Official Information Act requests Transparency Statement Make a request for official information\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"The Private Secretary\", \"scope\": \"medium\", \"claim\": \"The Private Secretary is a play.\", \"answer\": \"play\", \"quote\": \"The Private Secretary The Private Secretary is a 1883 farce in three acts, by Charles Hawtrey.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0161-f5c81f98257f:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"The Private Secretary\", \"scope\": \"medium\", \"claim\": \"The medium of \\\"The Private Secretary\\\" is a film.\", \"answer\": \"film\", \"quote\": \"The Private Secretary (1935 film) The Private Secretary is a 1935 British comedy film directed by Henry Edwards and starring Edward Everett Horton, Barry MacKay, Judy Gunn and Oscar Asche.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}], \"R5_answers\": [\"play\", \"film\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": \"film\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"play\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}]}"
